{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 480,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first discovery of extraterrestrial life \nwill almost certainly NOT be when it visits us,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5960,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nor when we visit it. It won’t be when we \nintercept it’s stray embarassing TV signals. It’ll be in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "excruciatingly faint changes in the color \nof alien sunsets glimpsed hundreds of light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years away. Today we’re going to talk about the \nfirst such hint, why it's probably not aliens,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25640,
    "dDurationMs": 5806,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but why there’s still a tiny chance that \nit still might not not be aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36960,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have a long and storied history of almost \ncertainly not finding aliens when some people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thought maybe we had. From the WOW signal to \nOumuamua, claims of alien origins have always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47560,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "given way to natural explanations once we thought \nharder about the phenomena. And the latest case  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53360,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a just maybe but really very much probably \nnot detection of life in the universe is this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59640,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guy—K2-18b—the extra-solar mini-neptune \nwhose atmosphere probably doesn’t, but just "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67880,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may not not have a tiny bit of a molecule-dimethyl sulphide-that on Earth is produced only by life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75560,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why is this case more exciting than the other nearly detected aliens? Well it's because the method by which we did this is probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how we’re actually going to find alien life in the first place — by observing the subtle effect of living  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90680,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organisms on the starlight that sifts through \nthe alien atmosphere as the hopefully-inhabited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97440,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet passes across the face of its home star. \nThat’s right seeing alien sunsets. This method and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "machine behind the current observations - the \nJames Webb Space Telescope - might well be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108840,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key to our first detection of extraterrestrial \nlife, even if K2-18b proves to be barren. And let me  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115120,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "add that an even better reason to talk about \nthis planet is that it's very "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121000,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interesting in its own right. But I will lead \nwith aliens because that’s what the kids seem to want."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128480,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s get to know K2-18b a bit better. \nIt was discovered as part of the Kepler  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mission. Which discovered 1000s of extra-solar planets - exoplanets - by watching for the rare cases where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139000,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these planets happen to pass across the face \nof their home star from our point of view."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144400,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The K2-18 system was identified in 2015. It’s \nrelatively close by, at a mere 124 lightyears.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153040,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The home star—K2-18a—is a dim red M-dwarf, almost \nexactly half the mass and radius of our sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161920,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a surface temperature of about 3500 \nKelvin, probably between 2 and 3 billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168280,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years old. Orbiting close to this star \nare two known planets, K2-18b and K2-18c."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175480,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know quite a bit about these planets \njust from the Kepler observations. By  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measuring the dimming of the star during a \ntransit, we get the radius of the planet. By  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measuring the time between transits, we \nget the size and duration of its orbit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get the mass of the planet itself we need \nan extra observation to see how the planet’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity tugs ever so slightly on its home \nstar, causing it to wobble—an effect only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198880,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visible in the tiny back-and-forth Doppler \nwavelength shift of the star’s spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From all this we know that K2-18b \n8.6 times the mass of the earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210760,
    "dDurationMs": 10200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 2.6 time its diameter. It orbits closer \nto its star than Mercury does to our sun. Its planetary neighbor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220960,
    "dDurationMs": 4332,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "KB-18c, orbits even closer \nand is a bit smaller."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225292,
    "dDurationMs": 5948,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now a planet’s proximity to its star tells us \nwhether water can reasonably exist in its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231240,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid form given the amount of light it gets \nfrom its star. We’ve done an episode on why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236320,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water is the best stuff in the universe in which \nto brew and sustain life—so for today let’s just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242600,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accept that when we find a planet it the so-called \nhabitable zone we have cause to be interested."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249040,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And K2-18b is I’d say marginally within its stars \nhabitable zone. Although it’s orbiting much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256600,
    "dDurationMs": 9480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closer, that star is much fainter than our sun. So there might be H2O in liquid form which means their might be oceans."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so how do we tell what this thing is made of? If we combine the planet’s mass with its size we get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its density. Sometimes that gives us a really \ngood idea of what the planet is made of. K2-18b  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a density less that half that of Earth. That \nmeans it's not all rock. It probably has a rocky  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interior, but there’s some sort of fluffier stuff \non top of that—material that puffs up the planet’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size so it creates a bigger eclipse crossing its \nstar, but which doesn’t weigh as much as rock.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The two broad alternatives could be a massive \nplanet-wide ocean or a huge amounst of gas or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305760,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps both. We’ll come back to the details on this. Now planets within this mass range are sometimes called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312840,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super-Earths, sometimes mini-Neptunes—depending \non whether they’re more rocky or more gassy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Given the possibility of liquid water, \nastronomers were excited to see what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323520,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "K2-18b is really made of. In 2019, we \npointed the Hubble space telescope at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329720,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet to find out what its atmosphere \nis made of. Now keep in mind that Hubble can in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335680,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no way see this planet. Its faint home star is \nstill vastly brighter than the planet. But we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can still see the signature of the planet’s \natmosphere in the light of the star itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you see our Sun’s light we're seeing \nit though the filter of our own atmosphere.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353760,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A lot of the shorter, bluer wavelengths \nof light are scattered away making it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358040,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more yellow. If you could tune your eyes \nto scan through infrared wavelengths,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’d see that the sun goes dark over certain \nwavelength ranges where much of its light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369200,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is blocked by molecules in the atmosphere. \nMolecules like H20 and C02 tend to vibrate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at particular frequencies. When a photon \nin these frequency ranges passes near such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381280,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a molecule it’s very likely to get sucked \nup to power one of these vibrations. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386040,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result is that the spectrum of sunlight viewed \nfrom the Earth’s surface has these deep dark gaps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392480,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we can do this for Earth then \nwhy not for other planets? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395920,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because we aren’t on the surface of those \nplanets. But remember, these Kepler planets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by definition pass right in front of their star. \nMost of the photons that the star shoots at us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "miss that planet entirely. A small fraction \nof them are completely blocked by the planet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412640,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is how we know the planet is there. And a \nyet much smaller fraction skim the edge of the planet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418440,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passing through its atmosphere and traveling \nto us. And a yet tinier fraction of those photons still try to pass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the atmosphere but happen to have \njust the right frequency to be blocked by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430360,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules in that atmosphere. So by comparing the starlight that passes through the atmosphere to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436240,
    "dDurationMs": 6818,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the starlight that doesn’t, we can figure out \nwhat molecules this alien atmosphere contains."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443080,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, although we don’t see this directly \nwe’re looking at the rim of reddened light sifting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450160,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the planetary atmosphere—the sum of all \nits alien sunsets and sunrises at that moment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is not exactly a straightforward \nprocess. On one of K2-18b’s transits,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Hubble Space Telescope observed this \nspectrum. By comparing this to how the star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looks when the planet isn’t in the way it was \npossible to identify some divots—absorption  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "features—caused by some of the light getting \ntrapped as it filtered through the planet’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479280,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere. The researchers noticed a \nsignature consistent with the atmosphere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483040,
    "dDurationMs": 8840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "containing water vapor. And this catapulted K2-18b to the then-most-famous exoplanet in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491880,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned that this planet had an ambiguous \ncomposition—perhaps it was a super-Earth—a large,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rocky world with a giant ocean. Or perhaps a \nmini-Neptune—in which case a lot more of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass would be in an enormous atmosphere dominated \nby hydrogen gas. The former would perhaps be more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509680,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exciting for the alien hunters because it’s \ncloser to the one place in the universe that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514600,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we know life did form—Earth. And the tentative \ndetection of water made that a bit more likely.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this water detection was indeed tentative. \nHubble is only able to see a little way into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrared spectrum, and so doesn’t catch the \nfull range of absorption features of water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Enter the James Webb Space Telescope, \nwhich, by design, is sensitive to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelengths 10 times longer than Hubble—it \ntakes us deep into the infrared spectrum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544120,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and gives us access to a much broader \nrange of molecular signatures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548040,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In September of this year, astronomers reported that they had made their first measurement of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553200,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere of K2-18b using the JWST. Using two \nof the infrared spectrometers, the Near InfraRed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561840,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imager and Slitless Spectrograph (NIRISS) and the \nNear-InfraRed Camera (NIRCam), astronomers found  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an abundance of methane and carbon dioxide in the \natmosphere of K2-18b. Those aren’t too surprising,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576840,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s plenty of CO2 on Mars and Venus and \nwe’ve seen methane on Saturn’s moon Titan."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583200,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about the water? Well, it turns out that \nthere isn’t any. At least JWST found no evidence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590880,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of water in the upper atmosphere of K2-18b. The \nabsorption features detected by Hubble turned out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597560,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be probably methane. Now before you roll our eyes and click to the next video, we’re not done with this planet yet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606040,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll come back to the water issue. But first \n- the reason K2-18b really got a bunch of media  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613040,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attention is that JWST saw a hint of something \npotentially even more interesting than water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620400,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Webb may also have spotted a funny little molecule \ncalled dimethyl sulfide—DMS. It’s basically a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "couple of methanes stitched together with a sulfur \natom. It smells like cabbages. In fact, it’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633880,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecule produced when you cook cabbage—although currently \ncivilization of cabbage fanatics is currently not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641040,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the favoured model for DMS in K2-18b. This \nmolecule is also a byproduct of bacterial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648520,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and phytoplankton metabolism. On Earth there are \nno significant non-biological sources of dimethyl  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655600,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sulfide—so if its present on our exo-world, \nperhaps we have our first alien biosignature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663640,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now before you crack open that when-we-find-aliens \nbottle of expensive champagne that you put away  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669280,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years ago, let me add a note of extreme caution. \nThe detection of dimethyl sulfide is at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677080,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1-sigma level. Normally to be confident in a \ndetection we’d want 5-sigma - which would mean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683340,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the detection is clear enough that it could \nonly arise by chance fluctuations in the data 1  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689760,
    "dDurationMs": 9080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 50,000 times. For a 1-sigma detection that’s \na 32% chance of that the signal could arise from random  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698840,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations. 1-sigma detections of very unlikely \nthings show up all the time, and most of them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706520,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn out to be false positives. That’s the case \nhere. There probably isn’t any DMS, but there also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might be some. And the latter is exciting enough \nthat we should keep looking. And exciting enough for me to dig  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little deeper into exo-atmospheric chemistry \nto understand what K2-18b might really be like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we should also do this because \nK2-18b’s atmosphere is interesting in its own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right. Observing molecules at the top of \nan atmosphere is only the first step in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding its nature. We can make models of an \natmosphere that include the chemical composition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740160,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density, pressure, and temperature \nall the way from the surface to space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plus the effect of varying radiation from \nthe star plus interactions with the surface,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748520,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on. With a given model we can compute the \nchemical reactions that can occur at different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layers in the atmosphere, and how material gets \ntransported between them. So if we observe, say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761920,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bunch of methane and CO2 in the upper atmosphere \nas we have in K2-18b, then the atmospheric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769720,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model we come up with has to tell us how those \nmolecules got there in the measured quantities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776880,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many atmospheric models were tried for K2-18b, \nand it so happens that the one that seems to best  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783800,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "match the methane and CO2 abundances includes \n… wait for it … a planet-wide ocean of liquid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791080,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water beneath an atmosphere of mostly hydrogen. \nIn that case, we don’t necessarily expect H2O to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make it to the upper atmosphere where we can see \nit. It’s reasonable to expect that it condenses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804880,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and rains out in deeper layers leaving the upper \natmosphere dry, just like the stratosphere on earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another interesting piece of evidence to \ndo with what we didn’t see with the Webb  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectrum. We didn’t see any ammonia. Ammonia is expected to be found in hydrogen rich  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmospheres of planets like this if the \natmosphere is actually big and fluffy. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s a huge ocean then we might expect less \nammonia because there would be less atmosphere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833680,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce that ammonia in, and because the \nocean can extract ammonia from the atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839320,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But remember, these are only models. Some \nresearchers claim that a water world beneath  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a hydrogen atmosphere is the best fit to the \nobservations, but there are other models that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could also fit the data, albeit not as neatly, and \nwe also can’t know if we’ve exhausted the space of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "models—the range of possible atmospheres \nthat could have led to the observations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regardless, astronomers have came up with a name for this type of world—an ocean world beneath  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867200,
    "dDurationMs": 9720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a hydrogen atmosphere—we call these now Hycean planets. And K2-18b’s status as the best observed candidate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876920,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for this new planet type makes it exciting \nregardless of whether it harbors life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883200,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that we have an idea of what the \natmosphere of the planet might be like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886880,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s think about whether it’s the \nsort of place we can even expect life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891160,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you travel down in an atmosphere both \nthe pressure and temperature get higher,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896240,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and on K2-18b the pressure might get so high that it’s beyond the critical point of water - the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902680,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point where the liquid and gas phases are no longer distinct. So depending on the mass of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908720,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atmosphere, there might even be no phase \ntransition between the atmosphere and ocean,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913400,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a smooth increase in density as you go down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916760,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These hydrogen-atmosphere’d worlds, \nespecially those with giant oceans,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are expected to reach pretty extreme pressures \ndown deep. But if you put water under too much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927160,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure it’ll form ice at any temperature. A \nsufficiently deep ocean on a hycean world should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transition to a layer of solid ice before you get \nto the rocky interior. And that forms a hard block  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940160,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the water and the planet, preventing the exchange elements with the upper layers. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946120,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure of the ocean alone could lock down \nplate tectonics. Together, these effects would stop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet from having the robust carbon cycling \nprocesses needed to support a stable biosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also hard to imagine where on \nsuch a world life would first develop.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On Earth the favoured candidates \nare deep-sea hydrothermal vents,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or perhaps tidal pools on the rocky ocean \nshore. Basically, a stable interface between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972520,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid and liquid with a strong energy gradient \nand ample raw material resources. But the “best  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fit” model of the interior of K2-18b doesn’t \nhave these ideal cauldrons for brewing up life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986320,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, so what next?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first and obvious thing is to stare at it more with JWST. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current observations, with the NIRISS and NIRCam instruments, only cover near-infrared wavelengths  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999280,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of about 1 to 5 microns. JWST’s other instrument, \nthe Mid-Infrared Interferometer - MIRI - goes all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way to 28 microns, the exact part of the \nspectrum it needs to see to disentangle the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectra of methane from dimethyl sulfide, \nand should be expected in the fairly near  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019000,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future. We just need to wait for it to pass \nin front of its home star one or two more time. By next year some time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it should be clear whether or not K2-18b \nhas DMS, and who knows what other surprise  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033480,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules might show up in the mid-infrared. If we do confirm the DMS then we need to work a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040480,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot harder to rule out all non-biological \nprocesses that could have produced it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047240,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if K2-18b doesn’t turn out to harbour \nlife, this is a really exciting time for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053360,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exoplanetary astronomy. We now have a way \nto probe planetary atmospheres with more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sensitivity than ever before. With thousands of \nknown exoplanets and the best infrared telescope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever built now working on them, we might \nbe close to answering fundamental questions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the types of planets and planetary \nsystems that populate our galaxy. And,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075360,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps, about the formation and abundance \nof life in the universe. It’s never aliens,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we’ll keep looking until it is. At which point \nyou can pop your champagne and order your new “ok,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089560,
    "dDurationMs": 6644,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was aliens” t-shirt, which we hope will one \nday grace the merch store for PBS Space Time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
